{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And when she is cleansed of her flow, and she counts, etc.</b> One needs to know the precise delimitation of the verse — what point does it intend? If the counting were meant, it should have said ״תִּסְפּוֹר לָהּ״ ('she shall count for herself'); and if ״אַחַר תִּטְהָר״ ('afterward she shall be clean'), it should not have said ״וְאַחַר״ ('and afterward'). It appears [resolved] based on what they said in Torat Kohanim, and this is their language: ״וְאִם טָהֲרָה מִזּוֹבָהּ״ — when her flow ceases; thus far. According to this, the precise delimitation of the verse is the word <b>מִזּוֹבָהּ</b>, and thus it says <b>וְאִם טָהֲרָה</b> — you should know from what time she is called cleansed of her flow: from the time her flow ceases she is called clean to count seven days. And this is what they said: 'when her flow ceases.' Understand this. <b>And you will find</b> that Rashi explained in the first chapter of Megillah (Megillah 8a), and this is his language: 'When her flow ceases, she counts immediately, for she does not need to immerse beforehand.' And this accords with what I have written." "<b>And when she is cleansed</b> — meaning, the flow has withdrawn, for such is the implication of the word ״טָהֲרָה״. And do not think that with the withdrawal of the flow the impurity has departed from her — not so; rather, only the impurity of the flow itself has withdrawn, and this is what it says: ״מִזּוֹבָהּ״ — but not the impurity of contact with a zav, and she is still impure and needs to count seven clean days. And this is what it says: ״וְסָפְרָה לָהּ וְגוֹ׳ וְאַחַר תִּטְהָר״ — she shall be cleansed from everything." "<b>Conclusion of the parashah of Metzora</b>"], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:57:20.764693167Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}